  "pref": "A confidently subtyped HNF1A-inactivated adenoma under 5 cm in a woman. EASL notes that subtyping may be used in some specialist centres to support longer intervals between imaging follow-up, and the molecular series found no malignant transformation in any HNF1A-inactivated adenoma under 5 cm in a woman (0 of 47). The usual technical objection — that subtyping is unreliable in small lesions — is not supported: in the series that measured it, subtype accuracy was 80% below 5 cm against 87.5% above (P=0.45) for imaging and 75% against 84.6% (P=0.48) for biopsy with immunohistochemistry, and lesion size did not differ between correctly and incorrectly classified lesions.",
  "avoid": "Do not let a reassuring subtype override size, growth, male sex or an exophytic position, and do not let an unreassuring subtype alone commit a patient to an operation that size and growth do not justify. EASL states that whether the haemorrhage or malignant-transformation risk attributed to beta-catenin activation is independent of sex, size and rate of change is presently unknown, and that prospective validation of imaging-based subtyping is necessary before widespread implementation. The 0 of 47 figure is also a resection-series denominator and is small: it excludes nothing above a few per cent.",
  "note": "This asymmetry — subtype may reassure but may not alone commit — is a local safety convention, not a guideline position. It is in the limited-evidence section for that reason."
 }
],

