  "order": "Ordered as EASL orders them — resection first line, the non-surgical modalities as alternatives reserved for poor surgical candidates. That order reflects the guideline's wording and the fact that only resection yields a specimen. It does not reflect a demonstrated survival, bleeding or complication advantage, because no study compares any two of these four in hepatocellular adenoma. What the full-text read does establish about the alternatives is mostly cautionary: embolisation left 34% of patients still requiring surgery, caused one reported death and did not change symptoms (P=0.134); conventional ablation achieved primary efficacy in only 57.8% of lesions and 31% percutaneously; neither provides tissue; and continued surveillance, the option EASL does not endorse above 5 cm, now has the largest supporting dataset of the four.",
  "flagnote": "Two local approval items. First: will the programme accept elective embolisation as a first-line alternative to resection in a good surgical candidate who prefers it? EASL reserves embolisation for poor surgical candidates. The 84% elective surgery-avoidance figure rests on 49 patients from multiple small series with no common protocol, and against it sit 10% grade D complications, one reported death three months after the procedure, 34% of patients still requiring surgery, unchanged symptoms at 3 months, no histology, and a pooled follow-up too short to detect transformation — a decision requiring explicit discussion of benefits and risks. Second: does the programme adopt a composite trigger — 5 cm, or growth of 20%, or exophytic position, or visible lesional arteries, or a segment II-III location — in place of diameter alone? <b>Correction:</b> the earlier draft asserted that the position and vascularity effects came out larger than diameter in one cohort. Those effect sizes could not be confirmed in the full-text read and have been withdrawn, so this item cannot be decided until someone reads that paper's positional analysis directly; what the paper does establish is a fitted cut-point at 35 mm. Registry: modality chosen, diameter change, complication by Clavien-Dindo grade, subsequent intervention, specimen or explant finding."
 }
],
