  "pref": "Every patient who has bled. EASL: residual viable lesion on follow-up imaging is an indication for resection (evidence level III, grade of recommendation 2), and further investigation once stable should be pursued to exclude malignancy and secure appropriate follow-up. In the systematic review of 851 patients, surgery was avoided altogether in 68 of 151 embolised patients (45%), so a substantial proportion need no second procedure — but that proportion is identified by re-imaging, not assumed.",
  "avoid": "Do not treat embolisation as definitive without the follow-up scan. In the 59-patient cohort, 20 of 59 patients (34%) ultimately required surgery despite embolisation, and one patient died three months after the procedure. Half the embolisations in the systematic review (95 of 196 tumours, 48.5%) were for non-bleeding lesions, so the 45% surgery-avoidance figure mixes acute and elective indications and should not be quoted as the post-rupture figure. Surgery avoided is also a process endpoint and the pooled median follow-up was 31 months — too short to find malignant transformation in a lesion that was never removed.",
  "note": "The interval before re-imaging is not defined by any study. A contrast-enhanced MRI at 6 to 12 weeks, once the haematoma has resolved enough to see the lesion, is the common practice and is a convention."
 }
],

