},
{
 "t": "Transarterial embolisation is more dangerous and less definitive than the earlier draft of this page implied.",
 "why": "A direct correction. The earlier version stated that no deaths were reported in either the 59-patient cohort or the 151-patient systematic review. The full-text read records that one patient in the 59-patient cohort <b>died three months after embolisation</b>, that Society of Interventional Radiology grade D complications occurred in 6 of 59 (10%), and that 20 of 59 (34%) ultimately required surgery anyway — so embolisation frequently adds a procedure rather than replacing one. Symptoms were unchanged after the procedure (P=0.134) despite median diameter falling from 76 mm to 48 mm at 5.5 months and 45 mm at 27.4 months, which means the benefit rests entirely on a surrogate: it changes the imaging more reliably than it changes the patient. A median post-treatment diameter of 45 mm leaves the typical lesion just below the threshold rather than eliminated, with no histology, re-entering surveillance. <i>Surgery avoided</i> is a process endpoint, the 84% elective figure rests on 49 patients drawn from multiple small series with no common protocol, the 5.3% major-complication rate pools emergency with elective cases so the elective risk is not separately reported, and a pooled median follow-up of 31 months is too short to detect malignant transformation in a lesion that was never removed. Given that 20-40% of resected presumed adenomas prove to be something else, an unknown fraction of those patients were embolised for focal nodular hyperplasia. None of this removes embolisation from the page; all of it belongs in the consent conversation, and approval item 5 should be decided with it in view."
},
