{
 "t": "The transplant indication rests on two registries, one of which does not record lesion size and the other of which miscoded a quarter of its cases.",
 "why": "Grade D, and the governance question behind approval item 10. In UNOS from 1987 to 2020, 199 patients were listed with crude waitlist mortality of 18 of 199 (9.0%) and 142 were transplanted at a median age of 34.5 with a median laboratory MELD of 8 — young people with preserved liver function — of whom 127 (89.4%) had no carcinoma; death occurred in 26 of 142 (18.3%) over a median 62.9 months, with 5-year patient survival 86.3% and graft survival 80.0%. Because the recipients were not in liver failure, almost all of that mortality is attributable to the treatment rather than the disease. 61.3% received MELD exception points although only 6.3% had glycogen storage disease and 4.9% a listing carcinoma codiagnosis, so the exception pathway functions as a discretionary route rather than a rule. And the registry records neither the number nor the size of the adenomas, so the largest experience of the most radical treatment in this disease contains no data on the variable the rest of the pathway turns on. The European adenomatosis registry found true malignant transformation in only 17 of 49 explants (35%) with three operative deaths, five retransplantations and three fatal carcinoma recurrences — roughly one patient in eight dying of the treatment or its failure, against a 2.5% ten-year transformation rate in adenomatosis managed conservatively — and 15 of 64 registry-coded cases proved not to be adenomatosis at all on verification. The accompanying systematic review of 99 published cases reports 6.0% mortality against UNOS's 18.3%, a threefold disagreement its own authors attribute to publication bias in their review. Neither dataset has a comparison group managed without transplantation, so the claim of excellent outcomes in well-selected patients is unfalsifiable from these data. The 2025 UK national guidance assigns patients to four eligibility categories by consensus with no cohort behind them and no reference to beta-catenin or sonic hedgehog status."
},
{
 "t": "Registry-coded adenoma data should be treated as unreliable until verified case by case.",
 "why": "Not a clinical position but a methodological one that bears on every number this page quotes from a registry, including the local protocol's transplant evidence and any future audit. In the European series, 15 of 64 cases coded as liver adenomatosis in the registry proved not to be adenomatosis once the contributing centres verified them — a quarter of entries wrong. The UNOS analysis depends on the same kind of coding plus a free-text search on the fragment <i>aden</i>, with no case-level verification, so its cohort definition is weaker than the one already shown to fail. The same problem appears clinically rather than administratively: half of presumed male adenomas are reclassified on expert pathology review, 43% of Dutch resections for presumed adenoma had their diagnosis changed, and 27% of referred sub-5 cm lesions in a prospective cohort were focal nodular hyperplasia. The implication for this programme's own registry is that the concordance field — preoperative diagnosis against final pathology — is not an audit nicety but the field that makes every other field interpretable."
