files.old:
   "Eisenhauer EA, Therasse P, Bogaerts J, et al. New response evaluation criteria in solid tumours: revised RECIST guideline (version 1.1). Eur J Cancer 2009;45(2):228-247. Cited here only as the source of the 20% diameter-increase threshold that EASL 2016 adopts as its growth trigger for hepatocellular adenoma; the criteria were developed for solid malignant tumours and have not been validated for benign liver lesions."
  ]
 },
 "provenance": "<b>Revised on 7 October 2026 after a full-text read of the depot.</b> All 46 papers behind this page were read in full and abstracted, each with the authors' stated limitations and an independent critique recorded, and every evidence string on the page was checked against that abstraction. Fifty-eight evidence strings changed, across 119 fields. Six are corrections large enough to change what the page tells a surgeon, and they are set out in the limited-evidence section: that 5 cm is a convention rather than a derived threshold, which the page now states in the size assessment itself and grades D; that the six-month decision point is mistimed, because only 8.5-15.3% of adenomas over 5 cm have regressed below 5 cm at 26 weeks against 58.5% eventually at a median 104 weeks with no complication during that follow-up; that the apparent size gradient in the 122-patient, 365-adenoma French series is largely a per-lesion artefact of a resect-everything policy whose sub-2 cm stratum is incidental microadenomas found inside specimens removed for a larger lesion; that transarterial embolisation caused one reported death and left 34% of patients still requiring surgery, where the earlier draft stated that no deaths were reported; that the 23.8% occult-carcinoma figure the earlier draft attributed to a US male resection series is not supported and has been withdrawn, the supported figure being 4 transformations in 27 male adenomas; and that the positional and vascular bleeding effect sizes the earlier draft attributed to a prospective imaging cohort could not be confirmed and have been withdrawn, with the composite-trigger proposal and approval item 7 left explicitly undecided until someone reads that paper's positional analysis. Grades were revised where the critique warranted it rather than from study design alone: the 5 cm resection trigger, the 6-month reimaging interval, the composite positional trigger and thermal ablation were all downgraded, and the pregnancy recommendation for lesions under 5 cm moved from B to C because zero ruptures in 51 pregnancies has an upper 95% confidence bound of about 7% and both pregnancy cohorts were pre-filtered by the rule they are used to relax.

Six papers newly obtained for this revision have been added: the French multicentre bleeding cohort that modelled size continuously and found no threshold (Ann Surg 2021, PMID 34334644); the Dutch nationwide audit of diagnostic accuracy in resections for presumed adenoma (Hepatol Commun 2022, PMID 36324268); the Korean nationwide clinical, radiological and pathological series (Liver Int 2024, PMID 39526315); the contemporary surveillance-dominant natural-history cohort (Eur J Gastroenterol Hepatol 2026, PMID 42214026); the narrative review presenting the hepatocellular adenoma and carcinoma classification of the WHO 6th edition (Virchows Arch 2026, PMID 42678415), which is cited throughout as a review of that classification and not as the classification itself, which has not been read for this page; and the imaging-surveillance meta-analysis already present from the first draft and now read in full (Abdom Radiol 2026, PMID 41364225). One paper named in the gap scan was not obtained and is not cited anywhere: McDermott 2021 (PMID 34687281). Elfrink 2021 (PMID 33478819), the Dutch nationwide comparison of 415 laparoscopic and open resections that clinical decision 4 describes through the Italian guideline, has since been read in full. Volume and page numbers were not available for the five newly added references and have not been invented; each is cited by journal, year, title and PMID, with that limitation stated in the reference entry.

Two references remain cited from abstract only, because they are not in the full-text depot: the review of ruptured hepatocellular adenoma management (Dig Surg 2010, PMID 20357452), which supports the acute-haemorrhage sequence in clinical decision 5, and the French-language review of hepatic lesions in altered liver vascularisation (Rev Med Liege 2025, PMID 39950525), which supports the vascular-liver-disease string in clinical decision 8. Both of those decision points should be read as the weakest-sourced on the page. Where the earlier draft carried a figure that the full-text abstraction does not contain and that figure bore argumentative weight, it has been replaced with a depot-supported figure or removed and the removal disclosed; purely descriptive detail from the abstract-only draft has in a few places been left in place, and anyone re-reading this page against the sources should expect to find such detail rather than assume it was verified.

The page was originally built from EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of benign liver tumours (J Hepatol 2016;65:386-398), which remains the last full guideline for this disease; the ACG Clinical Guideline on Focal Liver Lesions (Am J Gastroenterol 2024;119:1235-1271); the Italian multisociety benign liver lesions guideline of 2022, which the full-text read shows to be the only adenoma guideline built on a formal GRADE Evidence-to-Decision framework and which grades every size-based adenoma recommendation very low quality of evidence (D), conditional; the French multisociety guidance of 2021, whose every adenoma recommendation is graded C1, C2 or D2; the Nault molecular classification of hepatocellular adenoma (Gastroenterology 2017;152:880-894.e6) and the Rebouissou CTNNB1 genotype-phenotype study (Hepatology 2016;64:2047-2061) for subtype and mutation risk; and the primary literature since 2016 on subtype-specific risk, male adenomas, surveillance outcomes, embolisation, thermal ablation, pregnancy, adenomatosis and transplantation, all of it listed in the reference library with PMIDs verified individually against PubMed on 6 October 2026. The transplant assessment and quick reference 4 come from the local UTHealth Houston Hepatic Adenoma Liver Transplant Protocol V3, policy review date 21 September 2026, which is the authority for that assessment and for nothing else on the page; the board and front-door operating content draws on the UTHealth Houston / Memorial Hermann Master Workup and Staging CPG and its hepatic adenoma staging row. Allocation statements are reproduced from that protocol and have not been independently checked against the live OPTN Policy 9.4 and 9.5 postings or the December 2025 transplant-oncology review-board guidance; the protocol marks several of them VERIFY and they remain unverified here. The 2025 UK NHSBT Liver Advisory Group guidance has now been read: it assigns patients with benign liver tumours to four eligibility categories by consensus with no underlying cohort, and because it does not state how adenoma size, number, subtype or prior intervention map onto those categories, its criteria are still not paraphrased on this page. One earlier source gap is recorded for completeness: the file supplied as the European adenomatosis source contained an unrelated French conference paper with no adenoma content, so the adenomatosis evidence here comes instead from the Tours long-term observational cohort (J Hepatol 2019;71:1184-1192), the European Liver Transplant Registry adenomatosis series (Liver Transpl 2016;22:516-526) and EASL's own multiple-lesion section.

This page remains a draft. Unlike the five existing disease pages it transcribes no ratified system roadmap, because none exists for hepatocellular adenoma: outside the transplant assessment, every position on it is a proposal assembled from published guidelines and primary literature and awaiting multidisciplinary and institutional local approval. The full-text read has made the page more honest about the strength of its own recommendations; it has not made any of them policy. It is not active clinical policy, it does not replace clinician judgement, and every numeric criterion must be verified against the current source before use."
}

