 "pref": "Any indeterminate lesion in a patient with a known extrahepatic malignancy. The ACR incidental-findings algorithm names MRI as the modality for further workup in all four of its core recommendations, and the ACR Appropriateness Criteria make MRI without and with intravenous contrast the only option rated usually appropriate for a sub-centimetre lesion found on ultrasound in a patient with a known extrahepatic malignancy.",
 "avoid": "Do not overstate the margin. In a meta-analysis of 39 prospective studies and 3,391 untreated colorectal cancer patients, per-lesion sensitivity was 80.3% (95% CI 74.6-85.0) for MRI against 74.4% (68.7-79.3) for CT — overlapping intervals, no significant difference — and per-patient sensitivity 88.2% (64.8-96.8) against 83.6% (66.9-92.8), p=0.653, also not significant. The one real advantage is below 10 mm: 60.2% (54.4-65.7) for MRI against 47.3% (40.1-54.5) for CT, p=0.006. At 10 mm or larger there is no difference at all, 89.0% against 86.7%, p=0.617. Note also what the meta-analysis did <i>not</i> find: liver-specific contrast material, multisection scanners and dual-phase acquisition each gave no measurable benefit, so the case for MRI rests on the sequences and not on the agent.",
 "note": "Two cautions on transporting those numbers. They come from treatment-naive patients being staged for a known colorectal primary, where prevalence is high; the paper reports no predictive values at all, and after chemotherapy steatosis and sinusoidal injury degrade detection on every modality. And the figure that matters for a sub-centimetre lesion cuts against reassurance: the test being ordered to characterise it misses two metastases in five at that size. The same paper publishes two incompatible sets of per-patient sensitivities — 83.6, 88.2 and 94.1% in the abstract and Table 4, and 81.2, 93.4 and 94.2% in its own summary box — and does not reconcile them."
