 "provenance": "Revised on 7 October 2026 after a full-text read of this pathway's depot: thirty papers read in full and abstracted, each with the authors' own stated limitations and an independent critique. The revision corrected or qualified forty-one evidence strings, rewrote the prevalence quick reference so that every figure carries its denominator, downgraded fifteen evidence grades, added five papers, and roughly doubled the limited-evidence section. No recommendation was removed; the page's positions stand, and what changed is how well supported they are said to be. Built originally as a first draft from the UTHealth Houston / Memorial Hermann Transplant Oncology Master Workup and Staging CPG — a programme companion document that is itself a draft for institutional review, with its companion protocol set reviewed 20 July 2026 — together with the published imaging and benign-liver literature. The imaging backbone is the ACR Incidental Findings Committee white paper on incidental liver lesions on CT (Gore 2017, still the current version; no liver revision has been published and the ACR lists no liver topic under development), its 2010 predecessor, ACR CT/MRI LI-RADS v2018 Core and CEUS LI-RADS v2017 (both checked 6 October 2026 and both still the current diagnostic versions; the 2024 LI-RADS releases cover treatment response and ultrasound surveillance only), and the ACR Appropriateness Criteria topic Liver Lesion-Initial Characterization of 2020, now read in full as Chernyak 2020. The benign entities follow the EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of benign liver tumours of 2016 — still the last full guideline for haemangioma and focal nodular hyperplasia — with the subsequent literature used to say where that document has been overtaken, principally the 2022 subtype-stratified meta-analysis of hepatobiliary-phase signal. The LI-RADS evidence now includes the 2022 individual-patient-data meta-analysis of the major features and the prospective CEUS LI-RADS validation sub-analysis of 2024. Nodules in vascular livers are governed by the EASL vascular-liver guidance of 2015 together with the 2026 multisociety consensus on porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder. The at-risk fork and the diagnostic criteria for hepatocellular carcinoma follow the EASL hepatocellular carcinoma guideline of 2025 and the AASLD practice guidance of 2023. Say plainly what was not read. Six sources on this page are still cited from abstract or guideline text only and were not in the full-text depot: the EASL hepatocellular carcinoma guideline 2025 (PMID 39690085), the AASLD practice guidance 2023 (37199193), the three DEGUM contrast-enhanced ultrasound papers (19241506, 20740399, 25812115), the perihilar fine-needle aspiration series (21492336) and the fatty-liver imaging review (17102041). Every figure attributed to those sources is at the first draft's evidentiary standard. Two documents named in the gap scan were sought and not obtained, and are not cited here as read: the 2026 EASL guideline on vascular diseases of the liver, which supersedes the 2015 edition this page quotes and makes the whole of the vascular arm provisional, and the CAMINO study. Every PMID was verified against PubMed on 6 October 2026. This is a triage page: it starts at the scan and ends by routing to the HCC, iCCA, cHCC-CCA, CRLM, NELM or HCA pathway, and it makes no treatment recommendation beyond drainage of an abscess and the decision not to intervene on a lesion it resolves. Plainly: there is no ratified roadmap behind this page. No institutional decision has been taken on any threshold, interval or discharge rule in it except where the local CPG is quoted directly, every one of the sixteen local approval items in the approvals list is genuinely open, and every numeric criterion must be verified against the current source before use."
}

