 ],
 "order": "Ordered by the size and quality of the randomised comparison each regimen achieved, which is a real ranking here — unusually for this page — but a narrower one than the medians suggest. PRODIGE 24 is the only adjuvant trial to report an 18-month difference in median overall survival, and it did so against the then-standard, in a population restricted to age under 80, no cardiac disease, WHO performance status 0 or 1, full surgical recovery and postoperative CA 19-9 below 180 U/mL; nothing in it describes what to do for a patient who has not recovered from a pancreatoduodenectomy, and its 5-year disease-free survival advantage is 7.1 percentage points. Every comparator in this list is adjuvant gemcitabine monotherapy or observation, so the ordering is a chain of comparisons against regimens no pathway would now offer a fit patient, and the two preferred options have never been compared with each other. NCCN also notes how similar the single-agent arms of the older trials were to one another — 22.8 months in the gemcitabine arm of CONKO-001, 20.5 in the gemcitabine-containing arm of RTOG 9704, 20.1 in the bolus fluorouracil arm of ESPAC-1, and 23.6 and 23.0 in the two arms of ESPAC-3 — which is a useful reminder of how small the single-agent era's differences were, and a reminder that those arms were selected very differently on CA 19-9 and age.",
 "flagnote": "Local approval item 4: is mFOLFIRINOX the local default for every recovered good-performance-status patient who had no neoadjuvant therapy, given 75.9% grade 3–4 toxicity, a relative dose intensity of 0.80 or more in only 33.6% of patients, no significant benefit in the 101 patients aged 70 or older (HR 0.86, 0.53–1.39) and post-relapse survival numerically worse than in the gemcitabine arm — and what performance-status or recovery threshold moves a patient to gemcitabine with capecitabine? Local approval item 5: what is the local rule for adjuvant therapy <b>after</b> neoadjuvant therapy? NCCN says only that the options depend on the response to neoadjuvant therapy and other clinical factors, and ESMO states plainly that the benefit of adjuvant chemotherapy following neoadjuvant therapy and pancreatectomy remains to be established with randomised data lacking. No trial defines total duration, whether to continue the same regimen or switch, or what to do after a complete pathologic response — and PREOPANC-2's FOLFIRINOX arm gave no adjuvant treatment at all. Audit: regimen, cycles completed, dose intensity, time from surgery to first cycle, and grade 3–4 toxicity."
