 "order": "Ordered by the strength of the evidence in pancreatic cancer specifically and by how many patients the alteration reaches, not by response rate. Daraxonrasib is first because it is the only randomised phase 3 result here, and the only one with a survival endpoint — but its benefit is established in the RAS <b>G12</b> population that was its primary analysis set, and the non-G12 subgroup points the other way (HR 1.38, 0.60–3.17). Olaparib is second because POLO is randomised and double-blind but in 154 patients found from 3,315 screened, against placebo rather than continued chemotherapy, and positive only for progression-free survival with no overall survival or quality-of-life difference at interim. Everything below is single-arm, in rare alterations, with pancreatic subsets of 21 to 38 patients, no control groups, and in two cases no prespecified efficacy threshold at all.",
