  "foot": "Two operational points matter more than any single row. Order profiling and germline testing at the first visit and bank two extra needle passes at the index biopsy, because the commonest failure in this disease is a report that arrives after the patient is too unwell to act on it — and do not wait for the result to start treatment, since NCCN states that if BRCA1/2 status is unknown the patient is treated as clinically indicated. Then read the denominators. Apart from daraxonrasib and olaparib, every row here is a single-arm result in a pancreatic subset of between one and thirty-eight patients, usually drawn from a mixed-tumour basket trial, with no screening denominator reported, and response rates from baskets do not transfer cleanly to this disease; two of the rows — sotorasib's pancreatic cohort and the subgroup reads from the adagrasib basket — had no prespecified efficacy threshold applying to pancreatic patients at all, so there was nothing they could have failed. Read the germline row the same way: 5.5% is a prevalence estimate from a clinic-based case series compared with public sequencing databases rather than a recruited control group, carriage was not associated with survival, and of the six genes only <i>BRCA1/2</i> carries a randomised treatment consequence in this disease — so 5.5% is not the actionable fraction. Do not treat the gene as the target; different alterations in the same gene do not predict the same response, which is the point the non-G12 subgroup of RASolute 302 makes (HR 1.38, 0.60–3.17).",
