files.old:
},
{
 "t": "Disease-free survival in the open-label adjuvant trials this page relies on is partly a detection artefact, and the trial that showed it is the one this page treats as the weakest.",
 "why": "APACT is the only adjuvant pancreatic trial to use blinded independently assessed disease-free survival as its primary endpoint, and in the same gemcitabine arm, central review gave a median of 18.8 months while the unblinded investigators gave 13.7 months, with 77% concordance and 48% to 51% of patients censored. The investigator figure matches the gemcitabine arms of ESPAC-4 (13.4 months) and PRODIGE-24 (12.8 months), both of which used unblinded investigator assessment, as did CONKO-001 — where recurrence was dated from the first subjective symptom and assessed in part by abdominal ultrasound with no central review. That is why CONKO-001 can show a 2.4-fold disease-free survival difference beside a 2.6-month median survival difference. Every adjuvant disease-free survival hazard ratio on this page should be read with a five-month detection allowance."
},
{
 "t": "PRODIGE-24's 54.4-month median was an artefact of an immature database, and the mature data are a smaller result than the headline.",
 "why": "The trial was stopped early for efficacy at 91.8% of expected disease-free survival events, and with mature 5-year data the disease-free survival hazard ratio moved from 0.58 to 0.66, overall survival from 0.64 to 0.68, and the median from 54.4 to 53.5 months. Five-year disease-free survival differs by only 7.1 percentage points (26.1% versus 19.0%) against 11.8 points at 5-year overall survival, so part of the survival gain accrues after relapse — and post-relapse survival was numerically <b>worse</b> in the mFOLFIRINOX arm (13.1 versus 15.0 months), with chemotherapy at relapse reaching 66.9% of its relapsing patients against 83.0% of the control arm's, 74.2% of whom received FOLFIRINOX. Delivery was poor: 67.2% completed all 12 cycles and only 33.6% achieved a relative dose intensity of 0.80 or more. The population was age 18 to 79, no coronary disease, WHO 0–1, fully recovered, with a clear postoperative scan and postoperative CA 19-9 below 180 U/mL; in the 101 patients aged 70 or older the benefit did not reach significance (HR 0.86, 0.53–1.39), and the median age of this disease is around 70. Quality of life was never collected."
},
{
 "t": "APACT was a negative trial. Its overall survival result is a descriptive secondary endpoint read at the third of three successive data cutoffs.",

