  "pref": "Insulinomas are highly symptomatic and rarely malignant, which is exactly the setting in which NCCN and NANETS place enucleation. Ninety per cent pursue an indolent course. Where splenic vessels are involved by a larger tumour, splenectomy is performed; otherwise spleen preservation should be considered.",
  "avoid": "Do not enucleate a lesion within 2-3 mm of the main pancreatic duct, and do not enucleate a tumour large enough to carry nodal risk - see clinical decision 5 for the trade. And cost the fistula honestly: in a pooled analysis of 22 non-randomised comparative studies (432 enucleations against 716 standard resections), pancreatic fistula occurred in 25.5% after enucleation against 19.7% after standard resection, OR 2.09 (95% CI 1.41-3.11), p<0.001, and OR 1.93 (1.14-3.27) for clinically relevant grade B or C disease; the excess lost significance only in the four studies performing more than 20 enucleations in total and at least 4 a year. Vaccinate against pneumococcus, Haemophilus influenzae b and meningococcus at least 14 days before any operation that might include splenectomy.",
  "note": "A formal lymphadenectomy is not part of the operation for a benign insulinoma."
