  "pref": "NCCN states that its surveillance recommendations apply to cases where observation has been chosen: history and examination with multiphasic abdomen with or without pelvis CT or MRI at 12 weeks to 12 months, then every 6 to 12 months to a maximum of 10 years, then as clinically indicated, with chest CT as clinically indicated and earlier imaging if symptoms develop. Use the same modality and contrast agent each time so that growth is measurable rather than inferred - ENETS makes the same point explicitly, that imaging modality, contrast type, contrast timing and tracer should not be changed during follow-up.",
  "avoid": "Receptor-based imaging and FDG-PET are not recommended for routine surveillance. Do not let the interval drift: the trigger for resection in every one of the three cohorts above was measurable growth, which only exists if the measurements are comparable.",
  "note": "Grade D, and the borrowed schedule is now known to disagree with its own source. The published follow-up recommendations for completely resected gastroenteropancreatic tumours - a 22-member RAND/UCLA panel scoring 193 scenarios against an evidence base of 6 eligible studies plus two then-unpublished cohorts - recommend for a resected pancreatic tumour consultation with triple-phase CT or MRI yearly for 3 years, then every 1 to 2 years to a total of 10 years, deliberately de-intensifying the first three years relative to NCCN, ENETS, ESMO and NANETS while extending surveillance past ten. They also recommend no follow-up at all for a grade 1, node-negative pancreatic tumour smaller than 2 cm. That exemption is expert opinion at a 75% agreement threshold, in a dataset whose cumulative recurrence was 33.5% at 5 years and 48.5% at 10 and whose median time to pancreatic recurrence was 7.2 years, and node-negative in a small tumour usually means few or no nodes were taken. It applies to resected patients and not to observed ones, and this page does not adopt it - but the group should know that the schedule it borrows is contradicted by the paper it is borrowed from. ENETS 2023 sets a third schedule again, imaging at 3 to 6 months after curative resection, then every 6 to 12 months for 5 years, every 12 to 24 months to 10 years and then every 5 years, at level 5 evidence graded B, with patients told to expect at least 20 years and possibly lifelong surveillance. See approval 13."
 }
],
