  "note": "The 310-patient and 119-patient resected series quoted above are taken from the NANETS consensus text and are not in this page's full-text depot. The 305-patient Nordic study of advanced gastrointestinal neuroendocrine carcinoma that is in the depot concerns unresectable disease and bears on this assessment only through its prognostic findings: median survival was 11 months (95% CI 9.4-12.6) with palliative chemotherapy and 1 month (0.3-1.8) with best supportive care, but the untreated group was twelve years older with 52% at performance status 3-4, so that contrast is late referral rather than a treatment effect. Pancreatic primaries, 71 of the 305, had the longest median survival of any site - 15 months against 8 for colonic, p=0.01 across sites - and were more often receptor-positive (46% against 0-25%, p=0.001). Performance status dominated the final model: median survival 18 months at PS 0, 12 at PS 1 and 5 at PS 2, alongside primary site, platelets below 400 and a normal lactate dehydrogenase. There is no role for adjuvant therapy here outside a trial."
