 "note": "This schedule is consensus, not trial-derived, and the three consensus sources disagree with one another. NCCN starts at 12 weeks to 12 months and runs to a maximum of 10 years. ENETS 2023 starts at 3 to 6 months with a somatostatin-receptor PET, then every 6 to 12 months for 5 years, every 12 to 24 months to 10 years and then every 5 years, at level 5 evidence graded B - an inversion the GRADE framework it claims to follow would not permit. The RAND/UCLA follow-up recommendations for completely resected gastroenteropancreatic tumours deliberately de-intensify the first three years - yearly imaging for 3 years, then every 1 to 2 years to 10, with the decision to continue or stop discussed with the patient at 10 - while extending beyond 10, and exempt a grade 1, node-negative pancreatic tumour under 2 cm from follow-up altogether. That exemption came from a 22-member panel at a 75% agreement threshold, over an evidence base of 6 eligible studies plus two then-unpublished cohorts that carried no grade, stage or margin data - the very variables the recommendations stratify on. It is not adopted here. All three agree on one thing: no serum or urine biomarker has a routine role, and an isolated rise in chromogranin A is not an indication to change treatment without radiological progression. See approval 13."
