 "provenance": "Revised on 7 October 2026 after a full-text read of this page's reference depot. Twenty-eight of the 33 indexed papers were read in full and abstracted to a fixed schema recording setting, inclusion and exclusion criteria, results, the authors' own stated limitations, an independent critique and a bottom line, and every evidence string, number and grade on the page was checked against that abstraction. Thirty-nine evidence strings were changed and thirteen grades were downgraded; the assessment structure, ids, names and tone values are unchanged, five previously empty recommendation slots were filled from depot papers that the first draft indexed but did not cite, the limited-evidence section grew from 12 items to 25 and the approvals list from 10 to 15. Originally built from the programme-supplied extraction of NCCN Neuroendocrine and Adrenal Tumors (version not stated in the extracted text; its most recent references, including the MEN1 guideline of June 2025, place it at v3.2025 or later) as the spine, with the NANETS 2020 consensus paper on the surgical management of pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours (Pancreas 2020;49:1-33) as the main surgical source, and the ENETS 2023 guidance papers for nonfunctioning pancreatic NET (J Neuroendocrinol 2023;35:e13343), for functioning pancreatic NET syndromes (e13318) and for digestive neuroendocrine carcinoma (e13249). Grading follows the WHO 2019 criteria as reproduced in the NCCN pathology principles. Liver metastases are out of scope throughout and hand off by name to the NELM pathway, which carries the hepatic decisions and the primary-resection question in metastatic disease. What the full-text read changed most is the observation-versus-resection assessment. The ENETS position on the 1-2 cm band, previously taken from the abstract and from how NANETS and the ASPEN investigators report it, was wrong: the full text observes 1 cm or less, personalises 1-2 cm and strongly recommends surgery above 2 cm or with duct dilation, and the page and quick-reference table are corrected. Sadot 2016's inclusion band was 3 cm or less rather than 2 cm, so the paper most often cited for the 2 cm threshold never tested it; a quarter of its observation arm crossed to surgery and remain in it by intention to treat; the patients never operated were followed a median of 33 months against a median time to recurrence of 5.1 years in the resected arm; its metastasis-free survival advantage for observation is an endpoint artefact; and 51% of observed tumours grew. ASPEN's 20% rate of aggressive histology comes only from its 94 resected patients, a denominator selected for size, duct dilatation, age, anxiety and surgeon preference, and no comparison between strategies was performed; its metastasis rate is printed as 0.08% when 4 of 500 is 0.8%. In PANDORA, six of the eight progressing patients refused the protocol-mandated operation, so the protocol was not tested. Gratian 2014 is a National Cancer Data Base analysis rather than SEER, and the registry excludes tumours coded benign, which removes exactly the indolent lesions a surveillance policy concerns; its 33% nodal figure for sub-5 mm tumours rests on a 94-patient band in a variable missing in 45% of the cohort. The 2025 MEN1 best-practice recommendations contribute the only systematic comparison that exists: across 37 studies and 1,814 patients, surgery and surveillance for lesions under 2 cm showed no difference in metastasis or mortality at low to very low GRADE certainty. And Hashim 2014, Zheng-Pywell 2022 and Makris 2023 all draw observation-versus-resection conclusions from cohorts in which every patient was resected, which is recorded on the page as a structural defect of the nodal-risk literature rather than as a caveat. Still cited from abstract only, because no new papers were obtained in this pass: the AACE consensus statement on MEN1 of March 2025 (PMID 40232217) and the 2021 international consensus statement on the pancreas in MEN1 (PMID 32971521). Both are labelled as abstract-only wherever they are cited, and their numeric criteria must be confirmed before use. The NANETS 2020 surgical consensus and the ENETS functioning-syndromes and neuroendocrine carcinoma guidance papers were not part of the full-text depot and remain the programme-supplied guideline sources; a list of figures quoted from the NANETS text with no primary paper in the depot is recorded in the limited-evidence section and each is labelled where it is used. There is still no ratified Memorial Hermann roadmap behind this page and no alignment worksheet. It remains a draft, assembled from guidelines and primary literature, pending multidisciplinary and institutional governance review. It is not active clinical policy, it does not replace clinician judgement, and every numeric criterion on it must be verified against the current source before use."
}

