  "pref": "Kyoto 2024 uses a cystic growth rate of 2.5 mm per year or more as a worrisome feature, and that value has supporting data as a risk factor: subdistribution hazard ratio 3.83 (95% CI 2.14 to 6.86) for later pancreatic cancer. ACG makes growth of more than 3 mm per year a high-risk characteristic prompting a short-interval study. Fukuoka 2017 used an increase of 5 mm or more over 2 years. The European guideline uses 5 mm per year or more as a relative indication for surgery.",
  "avoid": "Do not present any of the four as derived. Kyoto 2024 states that reported predictive growth rates range from 0.96 to 3.5 mm per year and that it adopted 2.5 because that was the value <i>reported most frequently</i> — a choice by vote frequency among candidate numbers, not by performance. Fukuoka 2017's 5 mm over 2 years entered on two citations. ACG's 3 mm per year is given with no operating characteristics, and the AGA technical review it otherwise draws on had found that an increase in cyst size was not a statistically significant risk factor for malignancy at all. And do not apply any of them across different modalities, slice thicknesses or readers: none of the four has published measurement reproducibility, and 2.5 mm is within the range over which two radiologists reading the same cyst can disagree.",
  "note": "Downgraded to D from C after the full-text read: the threshold is a consensus value selected by frequency of publication, not a derived cut-off, however well the resulting variable performs in one cohort. There is also a signal the guidelines do not use. In the 804 Tokyo patients followed beyond 5 years, the increase in main pancreatic duct diameter over the first 5 years predicted later carcinoma (SHR 1.71 per mm, 1.44 to 2.03) while the increase in cyst size did not (1.46, 0.96 to 2.20) — and Kyoto 2024 considered adding 'increase in size of MPD' as a worrisome feature and rejected it for lack of evidence. Median duct increase over those 5 years was 0.2 mm (range −2.0 to 9.0), so this is a measurement that demands a consistent protocol. Whether this programme records duct growth as well as cyst growth is an approvals item."
 }
