  "pref": "Fukuoka 2012 said surgery should be strongly considered in young fit patients with a cyst above 3 cm, and Fukuoka 2017 that patients under 65 with cysts over 2 cm may still be candidates. The supporting observation is that risk accumulates rather than plateaus: cumulative incidence of pancreatic carcinoma was 3.3% at 5 years, 6.6% at 10 and 15.0% at 15 in 1,404 branch-duct IPMNs, and Kyoto 2024 states that its own option to discontinue surveillance does not apply to younger patients with branch-duct IPMN, because the risk appears cumulative over time.",
  "avoid": "Kyoto 2024 does not carry the early-resection recommendation forward, and no study has compared early resection against continued surveillance in young patients. Do not present it as the guideline position — it is a reasonable conversation, not a standard. Fukuoka 2017 also demoted cyst size as a surgical indication in its own text while keeping the 2 cm figure for patients under 65, so the recommendation is internally inconsistent at source.",
  "note": "This argument cuts both ways. The same young patient has the most years in which to suffer the long-term consequences of a pancreatectomy, and the most years in which a remnant can produce a new lesion: after resection of 130 non-invasive IPMNs the 1-, 5- and 10-year risks of a new IPMN were 4%, 25% and 62%, with invasive IPMN in 0%, 7% and 38% by 10 years."
