  "order": "Ordered from the most conservative to the most permissive. <b>No winner is picked here and none should be read in.</b> The three cohorts differ on four things, and those four differences account for the opposite conclusions, so the order reflects how much imaging each position commits the programme to and not any comparison of outcomes. First, baseline selection: the two cohorts that found a large excess admitted cysts that already had or went on to develop worrisome features — 36% of the US cohort had worrisome features or high-risk stigmata at some point, and the Tokyo cohort counted a mural nodule developing during follow-up as an outcome (subdistribution hazard ratio 11.5, 4.49 to 29.6) — while the cohort that found no excess excluded those cysts by design at entry and again at 5 years. Second, conditioning on survival: all three report a denominator of patients who reached 5 years without being resected or diagnosed, which continuously purges the cohort of its highest-risk members in whichever direction the authors are arguing. Third, the numerator: the two high estimates count high-grade dysplasia as an event against invasive-cancer-only registry comparators, which is what produces standardised incidence ratios of 18.8 and 10.8, while the low estimate counts invasive cancer only, and 14 of its 40 cancers were diagnosed radiologically or on aspiration rather than pathologically. Fourth, observation past the mark: the two high estimates watched for a median of 107 months each beyond 5 years, the low estimate for 85. Nothing about biology separates these papers that is not also explained by how they were built.",
  "flagnote": "The second central local approval item. The programme must choose a stopping rule and state it, because the three positions have different failure modes: continuing indefinitely commits imaging to patients who will die of something else, stopping at 5 years accepts a documented 4.4% rate of invasive cancer after that point in one referral cohort, and the age-and-size rule rests on two and three events with confidence intervals compatible with a threefold excess, in a cohort no guideline has endorsed. Decide also whether a patient who stops is told they may still develop a pancreatic cancer unrelated to the cyst, and whether a cyst above 30 mm is excluded from any stopping rule, since stable cysts above 30 mm carried a standardised incidence ratio of 10.29 in the cohort that proposed stopping. Audit: stopping decisions by rule applied, and any pancreatic cancer diagnosed in a patient after surveillance stopped."
 }
