 "why": "<b>Baseline selection:</b> the two cohorts that found a large excess admitted cysts that already had, or went on to develop, worrisome features — 36% of the 577-patient US referral cohort had worrisome features or high-risk stigmata at some point, and in the 1,404-patient Tokyo cohort a mural nodule developing during follow-up carried a subdistribution hazard ratio of 11.5 — while the 3,844-patient international cohort excluded those cysts at entry by design and again at 5 years. <b>Conditioning on survival:</b> all three report a denominator of patients who reached 5 years without being resected or diagnosed (363 of 577, 804 of 1,404, 1,617 of 3,844), which continuously purges each cohort of its highest-risk members in whichever direction its authors are arguing. <b>The numerator:</b> the two high estimates count high-grade dysplasia as an event against cancer-registry comparators that count only invasive cancer, which is what produces standardised incidence ratios of 18.8 and 10.8 — the US figure falls to 15.6 when restricted to invasive disease, and its abstract carries 18.8 — while the low estimate counts invasive cancer only, and 14 of its 40 cancers were diagnosed radiologically or on aspiration rather than pathologically. <b>Observation past the mark:</b> the two high estimates watched for a median of 107 months each beyond 5 years; the low estimate watched for 85. Nothing about biology separates these three papers that is not also explained by how each was built, and a programme choosing between them is choosing a study design, not a fact."
},
{
 "t": "The argument for stopping gets stronger with age only because the comparator gets worse.",

