 "t": "The three stopping papers reach opposite conclusions, and four structural differences account for all of it.",
 "why": "The stopping-rule studies differ in selection, endpoints and duration and cannot establish equivalent strategies. Oyama reported SIR 10.8 in the whole 1,404-patient cohort and 5.8 (3.7–7.9) in the 804 followed beyond five years. The 107-month follow-up figure is total follow-up, not time added after five years. Stable-lesion landmark cohorts also exclude patients who progressed or underwent surgery earlier. Consider competing mortality, surgical fitness and preference when choosing an interval or stopping rule."
