files.old:
   "WHO Classification of Tumours Editorial Board. Digestive System Tumours. WHO Classification of Tumours, 5th edition, volume 1. Lyon: International Agency for Research on Cancer; 2019. The source of the entity definitions used on this page — intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm, mucinous cystic neoplasm with ovarian-type stroma, serous cystadenoma, solid pseudopapillary neoplasm and cystic pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour — and of the two-tier low-grade and high-grade dysplasia grading that Kyoto 2024 and all three other guidelines use. Kyoto 2024 separates the former oncocytic subtype out of IPMN as intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasm on the basis of recurrent PRKACA and PRKACB fusion genes, which this page records but does not otherwise act on. Not re-verified against the current online edition for this revision."
  ]
 },
 "provenance": "Revised on 7 October 2026 after a full-text read of the whole 27-paper reference depot for this page, with each paper abstracted to its results, the authors' stated limitations and an independent critique. The first draft had been built from abstracts and guideline summaries; four of the five documents that set the thresholds on this page — the international evidence-based Kyoto guidelines (Ohtsuka 2024, PMID 38182527), Fukuoka 2017 (PMID 28735806), Fukuoka 2012 (PMID 22687371) and the original Sendai consensus (PMID 16327281) — had not previously been checked against in full, and reading them changed the page's spine rather than its structure. What changed most: Kyoto 2024's malignancy rates of 31% and 62% are prevalences in resected specimens and the document itself now states that the real rate across all branch-duct IPMNs is much lower without quantifying it, so neither figure appears on this page as a surveillance risk; Kyoto retained the 5 mm mural nodule and 10 mm main-duct thresholds in the same passage that reports odds ratios of 1.19 to 3.16 and 1.06 to 1.76 for them and an odds ratio of 7.90 for a 10 mm nodule; the 2.5 mm per year growth threshold was adopted because 2.5 was the value reported most frequently among candidates spanning 0.96 to 3.5; no sensitivity, specificity or predictive value is published for any risk tier three guideline generations in; surgical mortality and cost-effectiveness appear nowhere in the IAP line of guidelines or the European guideline, so those numbers have been assembled from ACG, AGA, PROPAN and the surgical series; the only large external validation found that the 30 mm threshold did not stratify risk (subdistribution hazard ratio 1.75, 95% CI 1.00 to 3.08, against 2.07, 1.38 to 3.10, at 20 to 29 mm); and the three cohorts behind the stopping debate are shown on clinical decision 9 to differ in baseline selection, in conditioning on survival to 5 years, in whether high-grade dysplasia is counted as an event against an invasive-cancer-only comparator, and in how long they observed past the 5-year mark, which accounts for their opposite conclusions. Two numbers flagged in the first draft as needing verification are now resolved from the full texts: the ACG 2 to 3 cm surveillance band is MRI or EUS every 6 to 12 months for 3 years then yearly for 4, and the Kyoto cyst-fluid glucose threshold is 50 mg/dL with 93% sensitivity and 89% specificity. Three claims in the first draft were wrong and have been corrected: Kyoto does quantify needle-tract and peritoneal seeding (0.3%, and 2.3% against 4.4%, P=0.403); Kyoto and the European guideline do give operating characteristics for serum CA 19-9; and the European guideline does not merely omit needle-based confocal laser endomicroscopy from its algorithm but recommends against it at GRADE 1C. Grades were lowered where the full text showed a threshold to be a consensus value rather than a derived one, and the reason is stated in each <i>note</i>. The current narrative review of the field (Gardner 2024, PMID 38442782) is cited only for its cost figures, its population-versus-individual framing and its list of circumstances in which stopping is uncontroversial: its table of branch-duct transformation rates pools structurally incompatible denominators into an apparent 3.5 to 5.5% agreement, and its guideline-comparison table misreports several criteria, so no rate and no criterion on this page comes from it. Three sources on this page were not in the depot and are not full-text verified to the same standard: NCCN Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma Version 1.2026, from the extracted text supplied to the programme with its Discussion dated 6 August 2026, which has no cyst algorithm of its own, cites Kyoto 2024 as the international standard, cites the superseded 2013 European consensus statement rather than the 2018 evidence-based guideline, and directs the reader to the most recent available guideline; the 2013 European consensus statement itself (PMID 23415799), listed because it is the document NCCN reaches for — outside the depot at this revision, it has since been read in full; and the WHO 5th edition entity definitions, used but not re-verified against the current online edition. <b>This is a first draft and there is no ratified roadmap behind it.</b> Unlike the programme's five existing disease roadmaps, this pathway transcribes no approved institutional document — there is no local pancreatic cyst protocol in the source set — and it takes no institutional position of its own. Where the four guidelines disagree, which on this disease is on almost everything that matters, the page shows the disagreement and refers the choice to the nineteen local approval items in the approvals section; the order of options in any assessment reflects guideline standing or the spine chosen for this page, never a demonstrated advantage. It is not active clinical policy, it does not replace clinician judgement, and every numeric criterion must be verified against the current source before use."
}

