 ],
 "pref": "Ampullary adenoma without intraductal extension. ESGE recommends papillectomy in this group (strong recommendation, moderate quality evidence), recommends en bloc resection of adenomas up to 20–30 mm to achieve R0 and reduce recurrence (strong, low quality), and recommends direct snare resection without submucosal injection (strong, moderate quality). NCCN agrees that adenomas without suspicion of adenocarcinoma, including those with high-grade dysplasia, may be safely removed endoscopically, and that this should be done at a high-volume centre.",
 "avoid": "Do not take on a lesion that cannot be reached or encircled, and do not improvise an en bloc attempt above 30 mm. ESGE suggests surgery when endoscopic resection is not feasible for technical reasons such as a diverticulum or size over 4 cm, and when intraductal involvement exceeds 20 mm — with surveillance still mandatory afterwards.",
 "note": "Submucosal injection is specifically not recommended: it lifts the mucosa away from the ductal structures the snare needs to engage and makes the anatomy harder, not easier. ESGE grades that recommendation strong on moderate-quality evidence, which the subsequent literature does not quite support — the 2025 European comparison of papillectomy with surgical ampullectomy notes that the evidence remains contradictory and that submucosal injection was still used in 24.2% of its matched endoscopic cases. The more useful warning about en bloc intent is that technical success and histological completeness are different things: in 798 Japanese patients, en bloc resection was achieved in 89.0% and technical success in 94.9%, yet only 58.6% of en bloc resections (416 of 710) were ultimately R0, the rest downgraded by thermal artefact or margin involvement. An en bloc resection is a better starting point for a margin assessment, not a guarantee of one. [EXPERTISE]"
},
{

