 "pref": "NCCN's high-risk features are equivocal or indeterminate imaging findings, markedly elevated CA 19-9 or CEA, large primary tumours, large regional nodes, excessive weight loss and extreme pain. Regimens differ by presumed subtype: CapeOx, FOLFIRINOX or FOLFOX for intestinal type; albumin-bound paclitaxel–gemcitabine, capecitabine–gemcitabine, cisplatin–gemcitabine or FOLFIRINOX for pancreatobiliary and mixed type. Repeat pancreatic-protocol CT or MRI afterwards, then operate if still resectable.",
 "avoid": "Do not offer it as a treatment effect. In the largest series NCCN cites — 8,688 ampullary cancers with 175 receiving neoadjuvant therapy — median overall survival was 43 against 33 months with surgery first and the difference was not significant (P=0.416), and the neoadjuvant patients were younger and more often node-positive. Neoadjuvant use runs at only 1%–4% of resections, downstaging occurred in 15%–67% of tumours, and NCCN states there is limited evidence to recommend specific neoadjuvant regimens off-study and that there are no prospective randomised phase III data behind these recommendations. Name the borrowing when you offer a regimen: modified FOLFIRINOX comes from PRODIGE-24, a trial of resected pancreatic <i>ductal</i> adenocarcinoma that excluded nonductal pancreatic tumours by design, restricted entry to WHO performance status 0–1, age under 80 and CA 19-9 under 180 U/mL, and produced grade 3 or 4 toxicity in 75.9% of patients — with no disease-free survival benefit in the 101 patients aged 70 or over (hazard ratio 0.86, 95% CI 0.53–1.39), who are closer to the median age of ampullary cancer than the trial population as a whole.",
 "note": "Surgery first is NCCN's preferred route for resectable disease. Where chemoradiation is considered because of positive or close margins, chemotherapy should be given before it. Nothing in the adjuvant pancreatic trials transfers to the neoadjuvant ampullary setting: PRODIGE-24 enrolled only after complete macroscopic resection, excluded nonductal tumours, and reported in a disease whose resected median survival is roughly half that of ampullary cancer. [PROTOCOL]"
