  "pref": "NCCN's lists. For pancreatobiliary and mixed type: fluorouracil–leucovorin, gemcitabine, capecitabine, capecitabine–gemcitabine, cisplatin–gemcitabine, FOLFOX or CapeOx, and modified FOLFIRINOX. For intestinal type: fluorouracil–leucovorin, gemcitabine, capecitabine, and FOLFOX or CapeOx. Chemotherapy precedes chemoradiation where chemoradiation is being considered for a positive or close margin. Only fluorouracil with folinic acid and single-agent gemcitabine have ever been given to ampullary patients in a randomised adjuvant trial, and that trial was negative on its primary endpoint.",
  "avoid": "Name the source of each extrapolation when consenting, and say that the two trials doing most of the work excluded this disease by design. Capecitabine–gemcitabine rests on ESPAC-4, a trial of resected pancreatic <i>ductal</i> adenocarcinoma in which nonductal pancreatic tumours were an explicit exclusion criterion: 730 patients analysed, median overall survival 28.0 months (95% CI 23.5–31.5) against 25.5 (22.7–27.9), hazard ratio 0.82 (0.68–0.98), P=0.032 — a 2.5-month median gain whose upper confidence limit almost touches 1, reported early on a monitoring committee's recommendation at 458 of a planned 480 deaths with no alpha adjustment presented, with the benefit concentrated in R0 patients (39.5 against 27.9 months) and essentially no separation in R1 disease (23.7 against 23.0 months), and grade 3–4 adverse events in 226 of 359 patients (63%) against 196 of 366 (54%). Modified FOLFIRINOX rests on PRODIGE-24, also resected pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma with nonductal tumours excluded, and with entry restricted to WHO performance status 0–1, age under 80 and CA 19-9 under 180 U/mL: 493 patients, median disease-free survival 21.6 months (17.7–27.6) against 12.8 (11.7–15.2), hazard ratio 0.58 (0.46–0.73), P<0.001, median overall survival 54.4 against 35.0 months, hazard ratio 0.64 (0.48–0.86), P=0.003, at the cost of grade 3–4 toxicity in 180 of 237 patients (75.9%) against 128 of 242 (52.9%), and with no disease-free survival benefit in the 101 patients aged 70 or over (hazard ratio 0.86, 0.53–1.39). Capecitabine rests on BILCAP in biliary tract cancer, whose own primary analysis was not significant (median overall survival 51.1 against 36.4 months, P=0.097), and on X-ACT in colon cancer for the intestinal subtype; cisplatin–gemcitabine on ABC-02 in advanced biliary tract cancer, which did include ampullary cancers but did not report how many and was not an adjuvant trial (11.7 against 8.1 months, hazard ratio 0.64, 0.52–0.80, P<0.001); and CapeOx on a 31-patient phase II trial in advanced small bowel and ampullary cancers of which 12 were ampullary, again not adjuvant. BILCAP and ABC-02 are cited here from their abstracts and from guideline text only, since neither full text was read for this page.",
  "note": "NCCN states that the panel added capecitabine–gemcitabine for pancreatobiliary and mixed type on the basis of clinical expertise and that there are no prospective randomised phase III data supporting these recommendations. The two pancreatic trials are therefore borrowed evidence in the strict sense: both were designed to answer a question in a different tumour with roughly half the resected median survival of ampullary cancer, both excluded nonductal tumours as a matter of protocol, and neither reports a single ampullary patient. Transplanting a regimen with 75.9% grade 3–4 toxicity into a disease with no randomised evidence that any adjuvant chemotherapy helps is a decision about risk tolerance, not an application of trial evidence, and the consent conversation should say so. For stage I disease NCCN offers systemic therapy or observation; observation is a real option, not a concession."
