 "pref": "NCCN defines good performance status as ECOG 0–1 <i>with good biliary drainage and adequate nutritional intake</i>, intermediate as ECOG 2 and poor as ECOG 3 — so drainage and nutrition are part of the eligibility assessment, not a separate matter. For pancreatobiliary and mixed type with good or intermediate status: albumin-bound paclitaxel–gemcitabine, capecitabine–gemcitabine, cisplatin–gemcitabine, cisplatin–durvalumab–gemcitabine, or FOLFOX; with ECOG 0–1 only, also FOLFIRINOX or modified FOLFIRINOX and NALIRIFOX. For poor performance status, palliative and best supportive care are preferred.",
 "avoid": "Do not treat any of these backbones as validated in this disease. ABC-02 randomised 410 patients with locally advanced or metastatic cholangiocarcinoma, gallbladder cancer or ampullary cancer to cisplatin–gemcitabine or gemcitabine, giving median overall survival 11.7 against 8.1 months (hazard ratio 0.64, 95% CI 0.52–0.80, P<0.001) and progression-free survival 8.0 against 5.0 months (P<0.001) — the ampullary subgroup contained 20 patients (9 cisplatin–gemcitabine, 11 gemcitabine), with OS HR 0.62 (95% CI 0.21–1.82), too imprecise to establish a separate ampullary benefit. The FOLFIRINOX and modified FOLFIRINOX options arrive from PRODIGE-24 and the gemcitabine-based ones partly from ESPAC-4, both of which were trials of pancreatic <i>ductal</i> adenocarcinoma that excluded nonductal tumours by protocol, and PRODIGE-24 produced grade 3 or 4 toxicity in 75.9% of a population restricted to performance status 0–1 and age under 80. NCCN states that data for systemic therapy in ampullary cancer are very limited and that the recommendations are extrapolated from pancreatic, colorectal and biliary tract cancer.",
 "note": "Subtype-directed selection partly extrapolates from colorectal, pancreatic and biliary cancers. ABC-02 did include 20 ampullary patients, with an imprecise subgroup OS HR of 0.62 (95% CI 0.21–1.82). ABC-06 separately included 11 ampullary patients after GemCis progression; it informs the subsequent-treatment discussion below, not first-line or adjuvant therapy. Screen for clinical trials and explain these population limits. [PROTOCOL]"
},
