{
 "t": "ESPAC-4 and PRODIGE-24 sit in this depot as borrowed evidence from a different disease, and the page now says so wherever it uses them.",
 "why": "Both are trials of resected pancreatic <i>ductal</i> adenocarcinoma in which nonductal pancreatic tumours were an explicit exclusion criterion, so neither reports a single ampullary patient and neither can be read as evidence in this disease. ESPAC-4 gained 2.5 months of median survival (28.0 against 25.5 months, hazard ratio 0.82, 95% CI 0.68–0.98, P=0.032), was reported early at 458 of a planned 480 deaths with no alpha adjustment presented, and its benefit sat in R0 patients with essentially no separation in R1 disease. PRODIGE-24 doubled median disease-free survival (21.6 against 12.8 months) in a population restricted to performance status 0–1, age under 80 and CA 19-9 under 180 U/mL, at the cost of grade 3–4 toxicity in 75.9% of patients, and showed no disease-free survival benefit in the fifth of patients aged 70 or over (hazard ratio 0.86, 0.53–1.39) — the group closest to the median age of ampullary cancer. Ampullary carcinoma has roughly double the resected median survival of pancreatic cancer and no randomised evidence that any adjuvant chemotherapy helps, so transplanting these regimens is a decision about risk tolerance rather than an application of trial evidence. NCCN itself says the panel added capecitabine–gemcitabine on the basis of clinical expertise and that no prospective randomised phase III data support these recommendations."
},
