files.old:
   "PERIPAN Consensus Guidelines, full document with all 38 consensus questions, 51 recommendations, evidence grading, comments and proposed actions, plus the PERIPAN pathology report template, published as supporting information to United European Gastroenterol J. 2025;13(7):1048-1068. The summary tables were used for this draft; the full supplement should be read before the page is ratified. Two process items promised in the PERIPAN methods are not reported anywhere in the published paper and should be looked for in the supplement: the independent Jury Committee's AGREE II quality score across 23 items and six domains, and the audience agreement percentages from the public vote at the Brescia meeting. Note also that 33 of 57 draft recommendations reached 80% agreement in the first Delphi round and the remainder were revised and re-voted until they passed, so the final figure of 50 of 51 measures iteration rather than first-pass agreement."
  ]
 },
 "provenance": "This is a second draft, revised on 7 October 2026 after a full-text read of the paper depot behind it. There is still no ratified Memorial Hermann roadmap for ampullary tumours, no alignment worksheet behind it, and no institutional position has been taken on any of the fifteen items in the approvals list — the page is a proposal for a multidisciplinary group to argue with, not a statement of practice. <b>What the full-text read changed.</b> Twenty-eight of the thirty-five cited papers were read in full and abstracted with their authors' stated limitations and an independent critique, and the revision moved the page in three material ways. First, ESPAC-3 can no longer be cited as establishing adjuvant benefit in ampullary cancer: the trial was never powered for its ampullary subgroup, reports no hypothesis test for ampullary-only survival anywhere, and the ampullary medians of 40.6, 57.8 and 70.8 months appear without a P value; its primary analysis was negative (hazard ratio 0.86, 95% CI 0.66–1.11, P=0.25), its adjusted analysis (0.75, 0.57–0.98, P=0.03) adjusted for four variables on two of which the observation arm was prognostically worse at baseline, and in its 96 intrapancreatic bile duct patients chemotherapy was numerically worse than observation. Adjuvant chemotherapy remains on the page and is now graded C and offered as a reasonable choice rather than a demonstrated benefit. Second, the subtype question has been rewritten as an asymmetric dispute rather than a tie: the reproducibility study is the ceiling on all of it (κ 0.39, all five observers agreeing in 23% of 232 cases, 40% of tumours mixed, pure intestinal only 6%, survival differing only after the mixed cases were force-assigned, and the subtype failing multivariable analysis at P=0.39), and of the two adjuvant cohorts the positive one allocated treatment at tumour board by prognosis with no adjustment of any kind and reports a whole-cohort result running the other way (hazard ratio 1.124, P=0.608) while the negative one propensity-matched on the actual selection drivers and handled immortal time bias — so the divergence is explained by cohort and method rather than by chance, although the negative result is still underpowered to exclude a modest benefit. Third, the European comparison of papillectomy with surgical ampullectomy is now read for its indication finding first: final histology was neither adenoma nor adenocarcinoma in 24.2% of papillectomies and 14.8% of surgical ampullectomies while advanced disease was found in 10.9% and 36.6%, so roughly a third of the procedures in both arms were done for the wrong indication; papillectomy R0 for adenoma falls from 80.2% to 65.9% once index-session ablation is counted as a probable R2 resection; and papillectomy failed in roughly two-thirds of T1 cancers. <b>Borrowed evidence.</b> ESPAC-4 and PRODIGE-24 are trials of resected pancreatic <i>ductal</i> adenocarcinoma that excluded nonductal tumours by design and report no ampullary patients; the page now says so wherever it uses them, as it does for the preoperative biliary drainage trial (cancer of the pancreatic head, plastic stents, no ampullary subgroup), BILCAP (biliary tract), ABC-02 (advanced biliary tract, ampullary patients included but not counted), X-ACT (colon) and the randomised evidence against extended lymphadenectomy (pancreatic). <b>Cited from abstract only.</b> Five papers were sought and not obtained for this revision. Three of them — the 91-patient malignancy prediction cohort (23442830), the EUS preoperative evaluation series (30834717) and the 14-patient ampullary neuroendocrine tumour series (27457864) — have since been read in full; the other two, the National Cancer Database node-yield analysis (PMID 38353072) and the intraductal ultrasound and transpapillary biopsy series (22319960), are still cited from abstract only, and every string that uses them says so. BILCAP (30922733) and ABC-02 (20375404) were also not part of the full-text read and are quoted from their abstracts and from guideline text. <b>Guideline sources, unchanged from the first draft.</b> The programme-supplied machine-converted extraction of the NCCN Ampullary Adenocarcinoma guideline, which carries no version string and which misstates ESPAC-3 on its adjuvant systemic-therapy page while stating it correctly in its discussion (see nonpm, and approvals item 10) — the full-text read confirms that discrepancy from the trial side; the NCCN Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma extraction for the margin, specimen and lymphadenectomy questions the two diseases share; the Brescia PERIPAN international multidisciplinary consensus guidelines of 29 July 2025 for the anatomic definitions, the subtype definitions, the biopsy and specimen-handling recommendations and the reflex mismatch-repair position; the ESGE guideline on endoscopic management of ampullary tumours of 2021 and the ESGE polyposis guideline of 2019 for the endoscopic and familial decision points; and AJCC 8th edition staging as reproduced in NCCN. Every PMID in refs was verified against PubMed on 6 October 2026 to confirm it resolves to the paper cited. Readers should know that nine of eleven main ESGE ampullary recommendations are strong on low-quality evidence, that almost every PERIPAN recommendation is low evidence or expert opinion with its promised AGREE II quality score and audience vote never reported, and that PERIPAN's 30% threshold for the mixed subtype has no stated derivation. <b>Where the sources disagree</b> — on whether subtype should direct adjuvant therapy, on surveillance intervals by Spigelman stage, on whether mismatch-repair and molecular testing is reflex, indication-driven or discouraged, on the minimum node yield, and on what to do with an invasive carcinoma found in a margin-negative papillectomy specimen — the page shows the disagreement and places the choice in a flagnote and in approvals rather than resolving it. Evidence strings carry both halves of every result. Grades fell in eleven places on this revision and rose nowhere, and each downgrade is explained in the note on the string it belongs to; no recommendation was removed, because the roadmap's positions stand and the page's job is to say how well supported they are. Grades are deliberately low across the benign end of the pathway: most of what is done there rests on retrospective series and expert consensus, and grade D is used where that is the truth. This page does not replace clinician judgement, and every numeric criterion must be verified against the current source before use."
}

