 "note": "Downgraded from B to C. The unpriced cost is neurological: grade 3 or worse peripheral neuropathy in 15% of the combination arm and <b>0%</b> of the gemcitabine arm, with 84% of those affected not improved to grade 1 or less at the primary cutoff and a median 195 days to improve by one grade — in a trial that collected no quality-of-life data at all. Only 287 of 432 patients completed six cycles against 310 of 434 on gemcitabine, 64% needed a dose reduction, and the population is the most selected in the adjuvant literature: CA 19-9 below 100 U/mL with a median of 13.6 U/mL, a clear screening scan, ECOG 0–1, and 1,226 patients screened to randomise 866, which is why the gemcitabine arm reached 37.7 months. The trial's most durable contribution has nothing to do with the regimen: in the same gemcitabine arm, blinded independent review gave a median disease-free survival of 18.8 months and the unblinded investigators gave 13.7 months, with 77% concordance and 48% to 51% of patients censored. Every other adjuvant trial on this page used unblinded investigator-assessed disease-free survival and reported 12.8 to 13.4 months, so disease-free survival differences in open-label adjuvant trials are partly a detection artefact."
