files.old:
 "why": "Two 2025 meta-analyses report 0.41 (0.31–0.53) and 0.41 (0.32–0.52) from substantially overlapping study sets, so citing both double-counts one evidence base. The first pooled only <b>univariate</b> hazard ratios by explicit design, reported I² of 12% from seven retrospective studies with different definitions of oligometastasis, and never mentions immortal-time bias; the second found I² of 64.5% in the same literature. The second's meta-regression is the finding: the relative rate of chemotherapy between arms explained 87.1% of between-study heterogeneity, roughly half the non-surgical patients received gemcitabine monotherapy against multi-agent regimens in the surgical arm, and the adjusted effect reached HR 0.18 (0.08–0.40) only where both pre- and postoperative chemotherapy were given, with no difference in any other scenario — an estimate fitted to 7 comparisons with 11 candidate covariates that its own authors call mathematical rather than empirical. The first review's own data say the same thing: a 32.5-versus-9.2-month gap generated by a RECIST response criterion, and the best outcomes in the whole review coming from 52 patients whose liver metastases had completely regressed and who had only the primary resected. Nationally, 891 of 47,785 patients with liver-only stage IV disease (1.9%) had the primary resected, only 137 (0.29%) had a metastasectomy, only 13.5% had preoperative chemotherapy, 85% had the primary removed without touching the liver metastases, and the whole surgical cohort's median survival was 10.74 months — no better than chemotherapy alone in unselected metastatic disease."
},
