  "pref": "The NETPET grade summarises the pair on the single lesion that is most FDG-avid relative to its receptor uptake, plus the burden of such lesions. In the 62-patient derivation cohort, median overall survival was not reached for receptor-positive, FDG-negative disease (P1) or for the mixed group (P2-4) and was 11 months for significant FDG-positive, receptor-negative disease (P5), p=0.0018. In a 319-patient three-centre cohort, median overall survival was 101.8, 46.5 and 11.5 months and median time to progression 25.5, 16.7 and 6.6 months across the same three strata (both p<0.001), with the score independent of histological grade on multivariable analysis (HR 2.376, 95% CI 1.682-3.357, p<0.001) and grade independent of the score (HR 1.615, 1.192-2.187, p=0.002). Inter-rater kappa was 0.8 and intra-rater kappa 0.9.",
  "avoid": "Do not use NETPET alone to decide an operation on a localized primary. The cohorts involved metastatic or unresectable disease, with only 39% and 36% pancreatic primaries and no pancreas-specific analysis. The validation centres had published earlier series, but patient overlap with those series is unquantified. Treatment was not adjusted for. SSTR-positive disease across P1–P4 may be eligible for PRRT; FDG positivity does not by itself exclude it, so treatment access cannot be attributed only to P1. These data support prognosis, not a validated treatment threshold or a localized-primary resection rule.",
  "note": "The SNMMI/EANM procedure standard makes the same point more cautiously: FDG positivity is not rare in G1 and G2 tumours and predicts progression and prognosis more strongly than tumour grade, and combining the two scans can identify the highest-grade, most aggressive lesion and direct a biopsy to it. What no source gives is what to do with the result in a localised primary. See the limited-evidence section. [EXPERTISE]"
 }
],

