{
 "t": "What to do with a receptor-positive, FDG-positive localised primary in a patient whose biopsy says G1 or G2 is not covered by any guideline, and is currently decided case by case.",
 "why": "The combined-PET score is now better supported and still inapplicable here. In the 319-patient three-centre cohort it independently predicted overall survival (HR 2.376, 1.682-3.357, p<0.001) and time to progression, with inter-rater kappa 0.8 - but histological grade was independently prognostic alongside it (HR 1.615, 1.192-2.187, p=0.002), so the claim in the first draft that the score outperformed pathological grading holds only in the 62-patient derivation cohort where grade was non-significant and no multivariable model was fitted. Both cohorts were metastatic or unresectable disease; only 39% and 36% of patients had pancreatic primaries and neither paper reports a pancreatic subgroup; two of the three validation centres supplied the derivation data; and treatment was not modelled, which matters because receptor-positive, FDG-negative disease is the eligibility criterion for radionuclide therapy. The 5-point scale has never been validated level by level and carries no treatment threshold. The SNMMI/EANM standard says FDG positivity predicts progression more strongly than grade and that the combination can direct a biopsy to the worst lesion, and stops there. Applying any of it to a 1.5 cm primary is an extrapolation with no outcome data behind it either way."
